    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Playing Island instead of Forest means they cannot cast Wild Growth on turn 2 (which requires G and enchants a land to ramp). With Forest, they could play Wild Growth turn 2 on the Forest, then have access to GG on turn 3 plus whatever other land they play. Alternatively, Forest enables casting Serum Visions only if they draw a U source, but Island enables Serum Visions turn 2 to dig. However, Terramorphic Expanse is also in hand and can fetch either basic. The optimal line is likely Forest now, then Terramorphic Expanse turn 2 (crack for Island), enabling Wild Growth on Forest turn 2 for strong ramp. Playing Island first delays the Wild Growth plan by a turn. That said, Island enables turn 2 Serum Visions which has value for finding lands, so this is debatable but Forest is generally better here for the ramp plan.",
      "actionTaken": "Played Island as their turn 1 land drop",
      "betterLine": "Play Forest to enable turn 2 Wild Growth (on Forest) for mana acceleration, with Terramorphic Expanse available turn 2 to fetch Island later if needed",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 10,
      "player": "Gem25F Timmy",
      "decisionIndex": 16
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Winged Boots is a cheap Equipment with excellent synergy with Galea (can be cast from top of library, provides flying and ward {4} protection). Ride the Avalanche is more situational and requires specific timing to be effective. Bottoming Winged Boots over Ride the Avalanche loses a consistently strong Galea synergy piece.",
      "actionTaken": "Put Winged Boots on the bottom, keeping Ride the Avalanche on top",
      "betterLine": "Put Ride the Avalanche on the bottom and keep Winged Boots, which is a cheap Equipment with strong Galea synergy (castable from top of library, provides evasion and ward {4} protection)",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 13,
      "player": "Gem25F Timmy",
      "decisionIndex": 17
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Playing Dimir Aqueduct as the only land on turn 1 means it enters tapped AND requires bouncing a land you control. Since Haiku Villain has no other lands in play, they must bounce the Aqueduct itself back to hand, completely wasting the turn with no land on the battlefield. This sets them back an entire turn compared to playing Island or Halimar Depths.",
      "actionTaken": "Played Dimir Aqueduct with no other lands in play, forcing it to bounce itself back to hand and leaving them with zero lands on the battlefield.",
      "betterLine": "Play Halimar Depths (enters tapped but lets you arrange top 3 cards for better draws) or Island (enters untapped, enabling turn 2 Dimir Signet or Mind Stone). Either option leaves a land on the battlefield and sets up future turns properly. Dimir Aqueduct should be played on a later turn when there's a land to bounce.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 20,
      "player": "Haiku Villain",
      "decisionIndex": 22
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Discarding Island when they have zero lands on the battlefield and only 3 lands in hand. Dimir Aqueduct can't be played until another land is in play (needs to bounce a land), so discarding Island leaves them with only Halimar Depths as a playable land on turn 2, making them very dependent on drawing lands.",
      "actionTaken": "Discarded Island",
