      "decisionIndex": 38
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Casting Wild Growth before playing a land is a sequencing error. The player has Sungrass Prairie, Forest, and Lumbering Falls in hand but only 2 lands on the battlefield. They should play a land first to ensure they don't miss a land drop, then cast Wild Growth. More importantly, playing Sungrass Prairie first (which enters untapped) or Forest would give them more options. With Forest + Island + Wild Growth on the Forest, they'd have access to GGU next turn. But if they play the land first, they can still cast Wild Growth with the Forest they already have. The main issue is casting a spell before making the land drop - if they play Lumbering Falls (enters tapped) or Sungrass Prairie, they still have G available from Forest to cast Wild Growth. Playing the land first is strictly better sequencing since it preserves information and ensures the land drop isn't forgotten.",
      "actionTaken": "Cast Wild Growth before playing a land for the turn",
      "betterLine": "Play a land first (Sungrass Prairie or Forest), then cast Wild Growth on Forest. This ensures the land drop isn't missed and provides better mana development.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 55,
      "player": "Gem25F Timmy",
      "decisionIndex": 44
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Gem25F Timmy's hand contains Fleecemane Lion (GW), Sungrass Prairie (needs 1+T for GW), Lumbering Falls (enters tapped, produces G or U), and Forest. The commander Galea costs 1GWU. Putting Wild Growth on Island gives the Island the ability to produce U+G when tapped. However, Timmy's color needs are primarily G and W - they already have an Island for U. Putting Wild Growth on Forest would give Forest the ability to produce G+G, which combined with the Island (U) and a Sungrass Prairie (GW) would provide better mana flexibility. More importantly, with Fleecemane Lion costing GW as the most likely next play, and Sungrass Prairie requiring 1+T to produce GW, putting Wild Growth on Forest means tapping Forest produces GG, enabling turn 3 Fleecemane Lion with Forest(GG via Wild Growth) + Island(U) + Sungrass Prairie(1,T->GW) - wait, that doesn't work either since Sungrass Prairie needs 1 generic. Actually, looking more carefully: with Wild Growth on Island, tapping Island gives U+G (2 mana), then Forest gives G, and if they play Sungrass Prairie (enters untapped), they'd need to pay 1+T for GW from it. For Fleecemane Lion (GW): Forest(G) + Sungrass Prairie requires 1 generic mana to activate. With Wild Growth on Forest: Forest gives G+G, Island gives U, Sungrass Prairie needs 1+T. Either way they can cast Fleecemane Lion next turn (Forest for G, pay 1 into Sungrass Prairie using the extra mana from Wild Growth). Actually Wild Growth on Forest is likely slightly better since it accelerates green mana which is more needed for the GW-heavy cards in hand (Fleecemane Lion, Ride the Avalanche has G). The difference is small but putting it on Forest is marginally better for the mana base.",
      "actionTaken": "Put Wild Growth on Island, producing U+G when tapped",
      "betterLine": "Put Wild Growth on Forest to produce G+G when tapped, since green mana is more needed for the GW-heavy hand (Fleecemane Lion, Ride the Avalanche) and Island already provides the blue mana needed",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 56,
      "player": "Gem25F Timmy",
      "decisionIndex": 45
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Ride the Avalanche is an instant that gives your next spell flash and puts +1/+1 counters on a creature when you cast that next spell. Cast during precombat main with no creatures on the battlefield and before playing a land, this wastes the card almost entirely. The player has Fleecemane Lion in hand but only 2 mana sources available after casting Ride the Avalanche (Forest tapped for G, Island tapped for U via Wild Growth). They can't cast any follow-up spell this turn. Ride the Avalanche's effect only lasts until end of turn, so the flash-granting and counter-placing abilities are completely wasted. The card is thrown away for zero value.",
      "actionTaken": "Cast Ride the Avalanche with no follow-up spell possible this turn and no creatures on the battlefield to receive counters.",
      "betterLine": "Play Sungrass Prairie as land for turn, then cast Sword of Hours (using Island+Wild Growth for {G} and Forest for colorless, paying {2}), developing the board. Save Ride the Avalanche for a future turn when you can cast it followed by a meaningful spell with a creature to target for counters.",
